# Caldera Colossus — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Caldera Colossus, the massive elite bruiser of the Slag
— a ponderous, columnar obsidian quadruped on four thick column legs, its enormous body
carrying an open furnace crater in its back that exposes a molten acid-green core — as a
3D voxel model with a rig a game can pose at runtime. Build it with `voxel-anim`
(painting opaque cells one operation at a time) as a rigged model that reads unmistakably
as this immense obsidian thing, walks believably on its columns, crushes on demand, and
breathes its furnace aura on its own. There is no target model to copy.

This test measures creativity and craft, not instruction-following. The brief fixes
what the Colossus is and how it must move; it deliberately does not hand you a parts
list, joint placements, or pose angles — working out the pieces a ponderous, crushing
creature needs, where they attach, and how they articulate is the test. Invent the rig.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It
  starts empty.
- x runs across the Colossus, `0`–`{{voxel.maxX}}`. y runs up, `0` (bottom, the ground) to `{{voxel.maxY}}`
  (top). z runs front-to-back, `0`–`{{voxel.maxZ}}`.
- Forward is +z: the Colossus faces toward `z = {{voxel.maxZ}}` (the front) at rest. Up is +y.
- Build the Colossus symmetric about the lengthwise vertical centerplane — the four legs
  and the two shoulder guards mirror left/right, and the body, the back crater, and
  its molten core are centered on it.
- The Colossus is deliberately **massive and columnar** — an enormous body carried on
  four thick column legs, filling most of the volume. It is the biggest thing on the
  field; nothing about it should read as low and long, top-heavy, or spindly.
- Each part is sculpted separately with `voxel-anim --part <name>`, in this same
  volume's coordinates, positioned where the part sits on the assembled Colossus (a leg
  already under its corner of the body, the crater already set into the back).

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Body — obsidian | `#241f2b` |
| Deep recess, shadowed seam — obsidian dark | `#16131b` |
| Accent plate (the accent region) | `#4a4358` |
| Acid glow — the furnace core and its seams | `#9ede3a` |

Do **not** use steel `#c9ced6` or violet `#b56bff` anywhere on the model. A game
paints those on at run time (see *The accent region*); baking them in defeats it.

## What the Colossus is (and what is yours to invent)

Fixed — the Colossus must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A **massive, columnar body** of obsidian, enormous and heavy, filling most of the
  volume. Its shadowed seams and recesses are obsidian dark.
- **Four thick column legs**, one at each corner, that carry it and walk (see the
  animations). They read as heavy columns — the legs of the biggest thing on the field,
  built for weight, not speed.
- **Heavy shoulder guards** over the front of the body.
- **An open furnace crater set into its back** — a broken cavity exposing a molten
  acid-green core that blazes from inside it — with **molten acid-green seams radiating
  from the crater across the body**. This exposed core and its seams are the Colossus's
  whole glow signature, and it is the **only** Slag with an exposed core.

The Colossus carries **no weapon and no tools** — its whole enormous body is its weapon.
It is the massive one with the exposed molten core: nothing about it reads as low, long,
fast, wide, top-heavy, or spindly.

Everything else is yours to invent — the exact silhouette and proportions of the body,
legs, and guards, how the furnace crater is broken open and how deep the core sits,
how the molten seams radiate across the body, and how you break the Colossus into rig
parts. Nothing here prescribes a shape; the test rewards a bold, characterful design that
is unmistakably the Colossus and animates convincingly.

## The accent region — a hard contract

A game recolors this model at run time to show the Colossus's **tier**, by finding every
voxel of one reserved color and repainting it. That reserved color is the **accent
plate** color `#4a4358`, and it is what makes the contract work:

- Sculpt the **shoulder guards** AND **the raised rim around the back crater** — and
  only those — in `#4a4358`.
- Use `#4a4358` **nowhere else** on the model. Not a stray voxel, not a highlight, not
  a seam, and **not** on the molten core or its radiating seams. Every voxel of it is
  treated as an accent plate.
- The accent must be **coherent contiguous plates** — the guards and the crater rim
  each a coherent plate group, not scattered flecks — and it must be clearly **visible
  from more than one angle**, so a player reads a Colossus's tier at a glance across the
  battlefield.

Everything else — the body, the recesses, the legs, the exposed core, its seams — keeps
its own color and is never repainted. Get this wrong and every Colossus in the game looks
the same tier.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with three required animation declarations by name (you author
the motion). Author each with `voxel-anim define-animation` then `add-keyframe`,
choosing the period and each key's `--interp` (`constant`/`linear`/`bezier` or
`ease-in`/`ease-out`/`ease-in-out`, with optional `--in-handle`/`--out-handle`) so the
motion has the right weight — the Colossus is immensely heavy, so its walk and slam are
ponderous and deliberate, never the light snap of a small creature.

- **`move`** — the walk (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`). Each thick column leg
  lands with weight and the whole enormous mass settles onto it, so it reads as an
  immensely heavy machine hauling itself along, not gliding or stepping lightly. Authored
  in place: the body does not translate across the scene — the leg cycle alone carries the
  stride (played on its own the planted foot slides straight back under the body,
  treadmill-style, then swings forward), and a game supplies the real travel.
- **`attack`** — the slam (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`). The Colossus rears
  and brings its full mass down in one crushing slam, then settles back to its stance
  before looping — a heavy commit, a recovery. The whole enormous weight throws behind the
  slam; this is a creature with no weapon crushing a target with its body. The feet stay
  planted.
- **`aura`** — the armor-aura idle (a **self-playing idle**, `auto_play = true`,
  `loop = true`). It runs continuously on its own, under everything else: the furnace core
  in the back crater pulses and breathes, and the molten seams swell and dim with it, so
  the armor aura the Colossus projects over nearby Slag reads as always on without being
  triggered. It **moves no leg** — only the core and its seams breathe.

You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top of this, but you must not
drop or contradict the required `move`, `attack`, and `aura` animations, by those names.

## Working the tool

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. Define your own parts with `define-part` (giving each a parent and a
pivot), sculpt each by selecting it with `--part <name>`, set pivots with `set-pivot`,
place joints with `define-joint`, and author the three animations' keyframes with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Run `voxel-anim --help` for the available operations
(setting and clearing single voxels, filling and stroking boxes, 3D lines, spheres, and
a mirror plane), the rig subcommands, and the animation subcommands, and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for each one's exact flags. Call `voxel-anim` once per operation;
run `voxel-anim render` and read `parts/<part>.png` (and the assembled `scene/*.png`
previews) between calls to judge each part against this brief — that each column leg
seats under its own corner of the body, the guards sit over the front legs, the
furnace crater is set cleanly into the back with the core exposed inside it, and the
animations read with the right weight. Run `voxel-anim render` before you finish so it
emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part scores
as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` renders one part; `voxel-anim render
--time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant to check the
motion).
